Acoustic Connect for Cordova

New Features

- Taps and gestures on page elements: a tap on an element of the app's page is
  logged as a click and a tap on that element, and a double tap, a long press,
  a swipe and a pinch as that gesture, as the SDK logs them on a native
  control: with the element's DOM id (or its path from the nearest element
  with one), its kind and its position. The plugin's page script listens in
  the app's main frame only, passively, and reads no text or field value.
  It needs the Connect Android SDK 11.1.39-beta or later and the Connect iOS
  SDK 2.2.35 or later, the plugin's defaults: with an older SDK nothing is
  logged, as with the `useRelease: true` defaults (Android 11.1.17, iOS
  2.1.51 or later) unless `AndroidVersion` or `iOSVersion` pins a version
  that has it. iOS: the
  default `AcousticConnectDebug` pod is now 2.2.35 or later (it was 2.1.51 or
  later)
- `PushEnabled` in `ConnectConfig.json` — the master switch of push. `true` by
  default (push has always been on in this plugin, so existing apps keep it;
  the Capacitor plugin's `pushEnabled` is off by default). `false` builds an
  analytics-only app: Android takes the `connect` artifact with no Firebase, iOS
  enables the SDK without push and adds no `ConnectNSE`/`ConnectNCE` extensions,
  no App Group or `aps-environment` entitlement and no `remote-notification`
  background mode (a plugin or `config.xml` that declares it keeps it). The
  `push.*` calls then reject with `ACOUSTIC_PUSH_DISABLED`
- iOS: the plugin adds the Acoustic CocoaPods spec repo,
  `https://github.com/go-acoustic/PodSpecs.git`, ahead of the CocoaPods sources
  in the app's Podfile, and keeps the sources that other plugins declare after
  it. CocoaPods trunk becomes read-only on 2 December 2026, and Connect iOS SDK
  versions released after that are published only to that repo. When
  `pod install` fails, the plugin runs it once more with `--repo-update`, which
  finds versions published since the spec repos were last updated

Fixes

- Android: the default Connect Android SDK is now 11.1.39-beta (it was 11.1.17).
  It brings the SDK's fix for an app that read as backgrounded while on screen
  and stopped posting (found in a Jetpack Compose app), and the SDK no longer
  drops a screen view UNLOAD that directly follows its own LOAD. With
  `useRelease: true` the default stays the 11.1.17 release, which has neither
  change yet: betas do not reach Maven Central
- iOS: the hook that rewrites the Podfile kept only the Connect pod and dropped
  the pods that the app's other plugins declare. It now keeps them, and restores
  the ones an earlier run lost from `pods.json`
- iOS: tapping a push notification that launched a killed app now runs its
  action (open URL, open dialer) in automatic push mode. The tap used to be
  lost: the SDK takes over notification handling only when your app calls
  `enable()`, which happens after iOS has already delivered the tap. The plugin
  now keeps the tap from launch and replays it once `enable()` has run, as long
  as that happens within 30 seconds of the tap. Nothing is kept in manual push
  mode or when push is off
- iOS: in manual push mode `push.didReceiveNotification` and
  `push.didReceiveResponse` now pass the notification or the tapped action to
  the SDK. They resolve `true` when the SDK took it and `false` when it refused
  it; before they resolved `true` without doing anything
- Android: `setCurrentScreenName` now only names the screen that later events
  are attributed to, as on iOS and as the README says. It used to log a LOAD
  screen view as well: an app that relied on that logs the LOAD with
  `logScreenViewContextLoad`. Log each screen of the page (a tab, a route, a
  view inside one) with `logScreenViewContextLoad` when it is entered and
  `logScreenViewContextUnload` when it is left, the LOAD of the next screen
  first, as the Demo app now does. On Android this needs the Connect Android
  SDK 11.1.12-beta or later, which the defaults are (11.1.39-beta, and 11.1.17
  under `useRelease: true`)


Known Issues

- Rich push cannot be end-to-end tested on the iOS Simulator — simulators don't display large icon.
- A swipe or a pinch that the WebView takes over to scroll or zoom the page is
  not logged: the page sees the touch cancelled. A gesture on an element that
  handles it itself (`touch-action: none`) is logged
- Typing inside the page is not captured automatically — call
  `logTextChangeEvent` yourself; it is sent as a custom event. Clicks are
  captured with the SDKs named under New Features; with older ones call
  `logClickEvent`
- Native layout masking rules do not mask text inside the WebView; use
  `ScreenCaptureEnabled: false` to keep page content out of captures
- `layoutConfigAndroid` replaces the Android SDK's whole layout config; the
  effect of layout rules on Android has not been verified
- On Android the SDK drops a screen view whose screen name is still queued, so
  `logScreenViewContextUnload` for the screen just loaded rejects with
  `ACOUSTIC_INTERNAL_ERROR`; unload the previous screen instead
- `logCustomEvent` values are nested under `data.value` on iOS but flat on
  Android, and a `logSignal` payload under `signal.data.value` on iOS but at the
  root of `signal` on Android; `logSignal` drops a top-level number on Android
  SDKs before 11.0.24-beta (the defaults, 11.1.39-beta and 11.1.17 under
  `useRelease`, send it)
- On iOS `logExceptionEvent` reports `stackTrace: (null)` and carries the stack
  text in `data.stacktrace`

Our environment for this release:

- Xcode 26.2
- MacOS 26.0.1
- iOS 15.1 to 26.x
- Android minSdk 26, SDK 36
